WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.125
Tokens per second approximates generation speed after processing.

00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:10.420
Time to first token includes request setup and prompt evaluation before visible output.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:16.282
Prompt-processing speed becomes especially important for long documents, code, and retrieved context.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:23.446
Latency describes how long one request waits, while throughput describes total work completed across time.

00:00:23.446 --> 00:00:28.439
A setting that improves batch throughput may increase interactive latency.

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:32.129
Multi-user services require queue and concurrency measurements.

00:00:32.129 --> 00:00:36.254
GPU offload can accelerate supported layers or operations.

00:00:36.254 --> 00:00:43.852
CPU fallback allows models to run when accelerator memory is limited, but it may reduce speed.

00:00:43.852 --> 00:00:49.280
The best offload level depends on memory, bandwidth, runtime, and workload.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:55.141
Thermal throttling reduces sustained clocks when heat or power limits are reached.

00:00:55.141 --> 00:01:00.134
Monitor performance over a long enough test to expose it.

00:01:00.134 --> 00:01:05.561
A short cold-start result can misrepresent a laptop or compact system.

00:01:05.561 --> 00:01:08.818
Benchmark end-to-end tasks, not only generation.

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:16.850
Include model load, prompt processing, first token, output, structured parsing, tool calls, and failure recovery where relevant.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.106
Record memory, power, temperature, and errors.

00:01:20.106 --> 00:01:22.060
Set practical expectations.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:31.829
Interactive writing may tolerate one speed, batch summarization another, and agent automation may care more about task success than raw tokens.

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:38.125
The right model delivers acceptable work within the user's patience and system capacity.

00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:40.947
Measure variability, not only averages.

00:01:40.947 --> 00:01:52.453
A system with acceptable average latency may still produce long pauses during large prompts, model switching, concurrent requests, cache growth, thermal throttling, or memory cleanup.
